      "outcomes": {
        "baseline": {
          "files_dropped_over_budget": 2,
          "files_read": 6,
          "findings": [
            {
              "category": "api-contract",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "file": "redis/_parsers/encoders.py",
              "line": 16,
              "message": "`Encoder.encode` now accepts `bytearray` (and the new tests assert `hset`/`hget`/`hmget` work with `bytearray` fields/values), but `redis/typing.py` is not touched by this PR, so `EncodedT`/`EncodableT`/`_StringLikeT` (and hence `FieldT`/`KeyT`) still exclude `bytearray`. In a PR whose stated goal is \"correct hints with all supported arg types\", the newly-supported type is still a type-checker error at every call site (e.g. `r.hset(name, bytearray(b\"f\"), bytearray(b\"v\"))`). Consider adding `bytearray` to `EncodedT`/`_StringLikeT`.",
              "severity": "minor"
            },
            {
              "category": "performance",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "file": "redis/connection.py",
              "line": 127,
              "message": "`HiredisRespSerializer.pack` now rebuilds the whole args tuple for every command and materializes `memoryview` arguments via `bytes(arg)`. memoryview arguments exist precisely to avoid copying large payloads (see the special-casing in `PythonRespSerializer.pack`/`pack_commands`), so this forces a full copy of every large value on the hiredis path; only `bytearray` needs the conversion for hiredis. Suggest converting only `bytearray` and skipping the tuple reconstruction when no argument requires conversion.",
              "severity": "minor"
            },
            {
              "category": "style",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "file": "redis/commands/helpers.py",
              "line": 22,
              "message": "`list_or_args` was re-typed from `(keys: KeysT, args: Tuple[KeyT, ...]) -> List[KeyT]` to `(keys: Any, args: Iterable[Any] | None) -> List[Any]`, which erases type information for every caller (`mget`, `sdiff`, `blpop`, `hmget`, ...). Since the motivation is that hash fields are `FieldT` rather than `KeyT`, a union (e.g. `Union[KeysT, FieldT, Iterable[...]]`) or a TypeVar would preserve checking instead of falling back to `Any` in a type-hint-focused change.",
              "severity": "nit"
            }
          ],
          "injected_tokens": 0,
          "memories_used": [],
          "retrieval_precision": null,
          "retrieved": 0,
          "truncated": false,
          "written": []
        },
        "memory": {
          "files_dropped_over_budget": 0,
          "files_read": 0,
          "findings": [
            {
              "category": "correctness",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "file": "redis/commands/core.py",
              "line": 9225,
              "message": "hmget's `keys` parameter is widened to `FieldT | Iterable[FieldT]` (FieldT includes bytearray/memoryview), but the implementation still delegates to `list_or_args`, which only treats `bytes` and `str` as scalars. A `bytearray` or `memoryview` passes `iter(keys)` and is *not* caught by `isinstance(keys, (bytes, str))`, so it is left as-is and then splatted into `execute_command(\"HMGET\", name, *args)` as individual integer bytes \u2014 the command is sent with one field per byte. The new tests assert the opposite behaviour (tests/test_commands.py:4689 and :4691, and the async twin), so they should fail for the bytearray/memoryview cases. Either extend the bytes-like check in `redis/commands/helpers.py::list_or_args` (e.g. `(bytes, bytearray, memoryview, str)`) or narrow the annotation.",
              "severity": "major"
            },
            {
              "category": "performance",
              "confidence": "low",
              "file": "redis/connection.py",
              "line": 127,
              "message": "HiredisRespSerializer.pack now materialises every `memoryview` argument via `bytes(arg)`, which copies the whole buffer and defeats the zero-copy path that memoryview arguments exist for (PythonRespSerializer deliberately appends the memoryview without copying). Only `bytearray` needs the conversion if hiredis already accepts buffers; additionally the generator rebuilds the args tuple on every single command pack, even when no bytes-like argument is present \u2014 consider converting lazily (e.g. only when `any(isinstance(a, (bytearray, memoryview)) for a in args)`).",
              "severity": "minor"
            }
          ],
